<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128625" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/20223734/Refreshment-Lamp-London-1890s.jpg" alt width="1000" height="1242" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/20223734/Refreshment-Lamp-London-1890s.jpg 1000w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/20223734/Refreshment-Lamp-London-1890s-290x360.jpg 290w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/20223734/Refreshment-Lamp-London-1890s-824x1024.jpg 824w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/20223734/Refreshment-Lamp-London-1890s-193x240.jpg 193w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/10/20223734/Refreshment-Lamp-London-1890s-768x954.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px"></p>
<p>Any reader of Victorian literature will wonder how much a ha’penny was actually worth in those days. For a little more than a year at the very end of that period, it could buy you a cup of coffee, cocoa, milk, tea, or “fluid beef” — which would pour out of a lamppost on the corner. The Pluto Lamp, as it was branded, made its debut at the Victorian Era Exhibition, which was held in 1897 to celebrate the Queen’s Diamond Jubilee. Amid such attractions as a 360-degree panorama of ancient Rome, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pepper%27s_ghost">Pepper’s ghost illusions</a>, and over 1,200 works of art by the likes of J. M. W. Turner, John Constable, and 180 specially selected female artists, a beverage-dispensing streetlight might seem mundane. Yet apart from being a technological marvel in its own way, it also presaged the technology-saturated streets one day to come.</p>
<p>Not long after the Victorian Era Exhibition, Pluto Lamps appeared in several London locations, including Exmouth Street in Clerkenwell, Leicester Square, and Rosebery Avenue. In addition to the illumination of their surroundings, and the drinks warmed by the excess heat thus generated, they in some cases offered postcards and four-to-the-penny cigarettes for sale as well.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>However much attention the Pluto Lamps attracted as a novelty, this was still the infancy of the modern vending machine, whose deficiencies had yet to be fully worked out. It proved impossible, at the time, to defeat the enterprising scoundrels who used round pieces of tin in place of genuine currency, and in any case, maintenance costs seem to have proven unexpectedly high. (To say nothing of the prospect of drinking out of an enamel cup on a chain, shared with the entire public, which surely put off even some Victorians.)</p>
<p>All of the Pluto Lamps were decommissioned before the end of the nineteenth century, but they’d done their bit to expand public ideas of what was possible in the urban public realm. “For over a century, lamp posts would again do little more than shine light on to the city streets below,” writes Lukas Neckermann in <em>Smart Cities, Smart Mobility</em>. “But by the early twenty-first century, the spirit of the Pluto Lamps returned. City infrastructure and street furniture would once again become multi-purpose, multi-use, entrepreneurial, and connected.” And indeed, though talk of “smart cities” may now sound as dated and unappealing as that of fluid beef, lampposts have more recently been given such side jobs as vehicle charging, environmental protection, and even crime prevention. That last brings back to mind the Clerkenwell Pluto Lamp, equipped as it was with a telegraph recorder connected straight to Scotland Yard: a device that, had it lasted longer, might have inspired many a Victorian novelist.</p>
